#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205190 :tutorial/content "Consider the multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^n \\to\\mathbb R$, defined by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf y) = f(y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n)\n$$\n\nwith $n$ independent variables, and $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb R \\to \\mathbb R^n$, defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}(x) \\\\ y_{2}(x) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ y_{n}(x)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich is a vector-valued function of a variable $x$.\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that the derivative of $f(\\mathbf{y}(x))$ with respect to $x$ is given by the multivariable chain rule.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y_1} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_1}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y_2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_2}{\\partial x} + \\dots + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y_n} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_n}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nThis can be written as a product of matrices as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂y_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂y_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that this can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf y}}_{1\\times n} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf y}{\\textrm{d}x}}_{n\\times 1}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf y}$ is the *total derivative* of $f$ with respect to $\\mathbf y$, which is a row vector of size $1\\times n$, and\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ is the *total derivative* of $\\mathbf y$ with respect to $x$, which is a column vector of size $n\\times 1$.\n\nIn the next slides, we will apply this matrix formulation to a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "17350522-fdcb-5adf-a0e3-30fbe076474e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12384 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205191 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ be two differentiable vector-valued functions. We want to compute the derivative of the composition $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}))$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$.\n\nThe **chain rule for total derivatives** tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{m \\times n} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{m \\times q} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}\n$$\n\nHere, the dot denotes matrix multiplication. The inner matrix dimensions match, so this is a valid matrix product.\n\n**Watch Out!** When applying the chain rule for total derivatives, the *order of multiplication matters!* For example, if we swap the order of the matrix products, we'd get an incorrect result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{m \\times q} \\qquad \\text{(WRONG!)}\n$$\n\nThis is incorrect because the inner matrix dimensions do not agree, making it an invalid matrix product.\n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "be71f9c1-7d65-5804-9536-88b35b9f95f5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12385 :tutorial/title "The Chain Rule for Total Derivatives"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205198 :tutorial/content "Suppose we define\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} + y_{2} \\\\ y_{1} - 2y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, we have $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nLet's see how to compute the derivative for the composition $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}))$.\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$. Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{f}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{y}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$. Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{y}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ is also a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nComputing our derivatives, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, by the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{dy} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2x_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2x_{1} & 2x_{2} \\\\ 2x_{1} & -4x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matrix gives us the total derivative of $\\mathbf f$ with respect to $\\mathbf x$." :tutorial/id #uuid "15c3d109-a5c5-5b01-a804-4e1b94e8269b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13107 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205199 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the following differentiable functions:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^p, \\qquad \\mathbf{z}: \\mathbb{R}^p \\to \\mathbb{R}^q, \\qquad \\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}^m\n$$\n\nThe chain rule can be extended in the natural way as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{z}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))) = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf z}}_{m\\times q} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf z}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y}}_{q\\times p} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}}_{p\\times n}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "7e23add8-cf11-5e3b-9950-84e432ab21da" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13109 :tutorial/title "Extending the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205217 :tutorial/content "Let's *verify* that our result from the previous slide is correct by computing the derivative directly.\n\nRecall that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} + y_{2} \\\\ y_{1} - 2y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, we substitute $\\mathbf{y}$ into $\\mathbf{f}$ to write $\\mathbf{f}$ explicitly in terms of $\\mathbf{x}{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} \\\\ x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative of $\\mathbf f$ with respect to $\\mathbf x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2x_{1} & 2x_{2} \\\\ 2x_{1} & -4x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis agrees with the result we obtained using the chain rule." :tutorial/id #uuid "06ee1fff-0a96-5071-a12d-9ca9bb198db8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14943 :tutorial/title "Verifying the Chain Rule Example"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205218 :tutorial/content "We can use the chain rule for functions that use matrices in their definition.\n\nFor example, consider the functions\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{B}\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^3, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^2$, and the matrices $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ are given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))\n$$\n\nevaluated at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_0 = [2, \\, 0, \\, -1]^T\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{B}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$. Thus,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} = \\mathbf{B}$.\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{A}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$. Thus,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = \\mathbf{A}$.\n\nSince these derivatives are constant matrices, the value at $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is simply their product. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}f(y(x)) &= \\frac{df}{dy} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n&= BA \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & 14 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f342078-4ed2-5d64-9c73-2ad6cd06661f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14944 :tutorial/title "The Chain Rule for Functions Defined Using Matrices"}]}
